{
 "problem": "Injected water carries its temperature. DES adds the injected enthalpy relative to the local temperature, which with fluid mass conservation makes its advective heat equation conserve energy. Two checks: radial hot-water injection against Avdonin’s solution, and a sealed doublet against an independent heat ledger.",
 "result": "Avdonin on the 0.1 m mesh: largest nodal error 1.50% of $T_{in}-T_0$ at 5.8 d (rms 0.28%). Doublet: stored heat follows the ledger within 0.019% after the first step, through breakthrough (producer at 78% of $T_{in}-T_0$).",
 "scope": "2D. Avdonin errors fall at first order with the mesh (0.4 m: 5.80%, 0.2 m: 2.84%, 0.1 m: 1.50%), as expected of the first-order (limited) advection scheme; time steps 200: 2.89%, 1000: 2.89% on the 0.2 m mesh. Thermal advection uses the Darcy flux at the start of each step, so a source switched on from rest loses part of its first step of heat when that step needs several thermal substeps: 0.75 of one step's injected heat on the 0.2 m doublet, 0.92 of one step's injected heat on the 0.1 m doublet; its share of the total falls as $1/t$. 3D check, a slab one element thick: largest error 5.75% (0.4 m), stored heat within 6e-06. (a) Avdonin profiles; (b) doublet ledger; (c) errors.",
 "meaning": "This checks the injected-enthalpy term with open outflow (Avdonin) and with outflow at a changing temperature (doublet), measured against energy conservation rather than inferred from the equation.",
